 "q_bbw_12": {
  "status": "ADVANCED, NOT CLOSED.",
  "ruledOut": "The `Firm` record is NOT the gate, at least not by itself. `AllowEquipmentContractFunctionality` = **Yes** at AF, on the same build as BBW, with the full menu structure present - and the root still does not render. There is also no `Equipment Contract Setup Page` field on FirmEdit.jsp at all.",
  "strongPattern": "The other 13 structures line up EXACTLY with the Firm `Allow X?` flags: Portfolio/Locations/Facilities/Contracts are all `Yes` and all four render; Program(Capital Programs)/Prototypes/Sites/Project(Opening Projects)/Parcels/Capital Project are all `No` and none render. So the Allow flags DO gate navigation roots - `Equipment Contract` is the single exception where the flag is Yes but the root is absent. A further condition exists.",
  "leadNotVerified": "/en/admin/SecurityPageAccess.jsp carries a per-User-Class page-access matrix (No Access / View / Edit / Delete / Default) with a dedicated row for `Equipment Contract` (SecurityFieldId_99), alongside rows for Contract (_97), Portfolio (_106), Parcel (_105), Prototype (_109). User-class page access is therefore a plausible second gate. **I did NOT verify AF's actual values**: on first load, before any user class is selected, every row shows `NoAccess` checked - including Contract and Portfolio, which do render. That snapshot is the unselected default and is NOT evidence either way. Confirming it needs a user class to be selected in the UI, which is an interaction I did not perform under the read-only rule.",
  "nextTest": "Select each user class on SecurityPageAccess.jsp at BOTH tenants and compare the `Equipment Contract` row, and compare BBW's Menu Structure admin (expect 14 structures there too). If BBW's differs from AF's only in page access, the gate is user-class security, not licensing."
 }
